ambit out of the face of Sneasler to let Kingambit's partners take care of it. Aerodactyl can OHKO Sneasler with Dual Wingbeat and set up Tailwind for Kingambit's other partners. Having Ghost-type Pokemon in the back such as Basculegion-M and Sinistcha as switch-ins to Sneasler's Close Combat is a good idea to cover for Kingambit's Fighting weakness. It is a good idea to have Garchomp and Kingambit on the field at the same time, as they perfectly cover for each other's weaknesses; Kingambit covers Pokemon such as Alolan Ninetales, Mega Froslass, and Mega Gardevoir, while Garchomp handles Pokemon such as Mega Charizard Y, Sneasler, and opposing Kingambit.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
-{"species":"incineroar","moves":["fakeout","flareblitz","partingshot","throatchop"],"item":"sitrusberry","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":32,"atk":0,"def":20,"spa":0,"spd":12,"spe":2},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:18Z","rationale":"Incineroar is the premier support Pokemon in all of VGC, using Fake Out to deny attacks and constantly cycling said Fake Out while also applying offense stat drops with Parting Shot and Intimidate to gain control of the damage on the board. Thanks to its good natural bulk and exceptional typing, it can switch in with ease on attacks such as the powerful Ghost-type onslaught from the likes of Aegislash and Mega Gengar as well as Fire-types like Mega Charizard Y. Flare Blitz: Flare Blitz allows Incineroar to make use of its high Attack stat, preventing foes from ignoring it especially when the sun is up. Flare Blitz notably threatens Kingambit, which usually scares away Incineroar due to Defiant. Throat Chop versus Darkest Lariat: Throat Chop deals a decent chunk of damage while also preventing sound-based moves; this means common moves such as Sylveon's and Primarina's Hyper Voice and opposing Incineroar's Parting Shot are blocked. Darkest Lariat, on the other hand, ignores Archaludon's Stamina boosts, though that is rather irrelevant in most matchups; the most enticing part of Darkest Lariat is its higher Base Power, allowing Incineroar to always 2HKO Shield forme Aegislash. Parting Shot: Parting Shot allows Incineroar to continuously lower foes' damage. Using Parting Shot also typically allows Incineroar to switch out last due to its low Speed, allowing frailer allies like Sneasler to come in safely. Other Options Protect: By dropping one of Incineroar's STAB attacks, Protect allows it to better position itself around the board. This is almost exclusively seen on Perish trap teams, where Incineroar plays a key role of in using Fake Out and Protect to stall Perish Song turns and then using Parting Shot to pivot around. Will-O-Wisp: Will-O-Wisp allows Incineroar to further weaken physical attacks on top of Intimidate and allows Incineroar to cripple Pokemon immune to Intimidate, namely Defiant Kingambit and Inner Focus Dragonite. Taunt: Taunt notably allows Incineroar to deny Trick Room from Farigiraf and Sinistcha, as well as crippling Pokemon that thrive off of their status moves, especially Whimsicott and Sableye. Items Sitrus Berry: Sitrus Berry grants Incineroar more sustainability, allowing it to avoid a 2HKO from Modest Mega Dragonite's Hurricane and almost always avoiding a 2HKO from Timid offensive Mega Floette's Moonblast. Chople Berry: While this is mostly used when Sitrus Berry has already been taken, Chople Berry still has its uses; most notably, it allows Incineroar to survive Close Combat from Sneasler, though only once. Abilities Intimidate: Intimidate is what makes Incineroar so strong; it can switch in, drop the foes' Attack stat, switch out, and repeat that same process over and over again. SP Spreads 32 HP / 20 Def / 12 SpD / 2 Spe with Careful: With the given bulk, Incineroar, after an Intimidate, survives Wave Crash from Adamant Adaptability Basculegion-M. Additionally, it avoids a 2HKO from Timid Mega Gengar's Sludge Bomb, and it survives Hyper Beam from Sylveon and Light of Ruin from Mega Floette. The Speed investment allows Incineroar to outspeed non-invested Tyranitar, possibly letting it get off a crucial Parting Shot. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Incineroar is very prone to the rain teams that plague the format, where Pokemon such as Pelipper, Politoed, and Basculegion-M can all OHKO Incineroar when they're in the rain. Incineroar also struggles against White Herb Sneasler and the less popular White Herb Garchomp, since thanks to White Herb, they can ignore Intimidate and fire back with Close Combat and Stomping Tantrum or Earthquake, respectively. Incineroar also struggles against Rock-type Pokemon due to its inability to damage them hard; these Rock-type Pokemon include Mega Tyranitar, Mega Aerodactyl, Mega Glimmora, and their base formes. Good Partners and Synergies: Sinistcha is a great defensive partner for Incineroar, switching into the many Fighting-, Ground-, and Water-type moves that would threaten Incineroar, while Incineroar can switch into the Fire-, Dark-, and Ghost-type attacks that threaten Sinistcha. Sinistcha also provides Trick Room for Incineroar to circumvent its relatively low Speed. Offensively, Basculegion-M threatens the many Rock-types that beat Incineroar and opposing Fire-types as well as walling most Sneasler out entirely. Mega Gengar can do the same to Sneasler, while Mega Aerodactyl can outspeed and OHKO Sneasler with Dual Wingbeat. Mega Charizard Y can prevent Pelipper and Politoed from hitting Incineroar with a super effective Weather Ball and can threaten Water-types with Solar Beam. Incineroar also functions well on Perish trap teams, working as a slow pivot that can use Fake Out to prevent foes from doing much while their Perish Song counters ticks down. How to Overcome Incineroar\u2019s Weaknesses: Bringing Pokemon that heavily benefit from the support Incineroar provides, such as Calm Mind Mega Floette and Mega Charizard Y, allows you to use it to its full potential; these Pokemon deal huge damage, compensating for Incineroar's passivity and allowing your team to break through would-be threats like Garchomp and Archaludon. Building your team with some way to circumvent rain is also advised, as this lets Incineroar avoid the powerful Water-type Weather Ball from Pelipper. Mega Charizard Y is the most effective, as it also matches up well against Sneasler that decide to forego Rock Slide; however, Incineroar doesn't typically fit on Mega Charizard Y structures due to stacking of Fire-types, so alternative weathers from the likes of Mega Froslass and Mega Tyranitar can also work.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
