t, but Whimsicott can bring Garchomp in range of Weather Ball with Moonblast. Garchomp itself is also a great partner, as it has a very good matchup against Mega Charizard Y and Mega Delphox, which Whimsicott struggles with. How to Overcome Whimsicott's Weaknesses: When using Whimsicott, well-placed Encores can get its team out of many bad positions. Leveraging Whimsicott's presence on the field with Protect is also important for this, as the opponent may feel locked out of several moves while Whimsicott is present. Moves such as Aqua Jet and Aurora Veil, as well as Earth Power from Venusaur, are examples of options that require positioning around Encore. Certain moves are only used in specific situations, such as when Venusaur uses Earth Power against Glimmora or when Aqua Jet picks off a foe at low HP, while others, such as Protect, can render a Pokemon useless if it is forced to use them multiple turns in a row. As such, these moves require much more precise positioning when Whimsicott is on the field. Leveraging this pressure alongside Whimsicott's Protect and the opportunity cost of manually switching can exponentially increase Whimsicott's usefulness. The partners mentioned in the previous section can help cover Whimsicott's common switch-ins, with Garchomp beating Incineroar using Earthquake or Stomping Tantrum as well as Mega Delphox and Mega Charizard Y using Rock Slide. When facing opposing Tailwind teams, using defensive options such as an allied Incineroar or bulky Pokemon may be more consistent than attempting to win Speed ties against opposing Whimsicott and lock it into Tailwind.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
-{"species":"pelipper","moves":["hurricane","protect","tailwind","weatherball"],"item":"sitrusberry","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":31,"atk":0,"def":14,"spa":0,"spd":18,"spe":3},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:19Z","variants":[{"hp":31,"atk":0,"def":14,"spa":2,"spd":16,"spe":3}],"rationale":"Pelipper is the premier rain setter of Regulation M-A. Being on the slower side, it is able to lead against opposing weather setters like Alolan Ninetales and have the rain override whatever the opposing weather is. Thanks to its Flying typing, it can threaten Grass-types like Sinistcha that otherwise spell trouble for rain teams. Weather Ball: Weather Ball threatens foes like Incineroar in rain, though it is useful in other weathers as well. For example, if the snow is set up, Weather Ball is capable of OHKOing opposing Garchomp. Hurricane: Hurricane is fully accurate in the rain, putting pressure on Grass-types like Sinistcha. Pelipper must be wary of using Hurricane, however, as it becomes rather unreliable if an opposing weather setter overrides the rain with their weather. It is also possible to use Hurricane to fish for confusion for a win, though this isn't to be relied on unless it is the only way out. Protect versus Wide Guard: Protect allows Pelipper to better position itself, setting up the rain more easily. Wide Guard, on the other hand, protects Pelipper and its partners from powerful spread moves such as Rock Slide from Mega Aerodactyl and Dazzling Gleam from Mega Floette. Weather Wars: Because of the abundance of weather within Regulation M-A, you must be careful when using Pelipper. Keeping it in the back to obtain weather control late-game will make it far easier to win, while leading off with your weather and forcing the opposing weather setter to swap in can leave them vulnerable. Items Sitrus Berry: Sitrus Berry allows Pelipper to nearly always avoid being 2HKOed by Dazzling Gleam from Timid Mega Floette and Wave Crash from Adamant Adaptability Basculegion-M in the rain. Abilities Drizzle: Drizzle enables partners like Archaludon and Basculegion-M while also shutting down opposing weather from the likes of Mega Charizard Y and Mega Tyranitar. SP Spreads 31 HP / 14 Def / 18 SpD / 3 Spe with Calm: The given SP spread ensures Pelipper survives Moonblast from Mega Floette or 150-Base Power Last Respects from Jolly Basculegion-M while giving it a good chance to survive Power Gem from Timid Glimmora. The Speed SPs allow Pelipper to outrun uninvested Corviknight. 31 HP / 14 Def / 2 SpA / 16 SpD / 3 Spe with Modest: The Speed and Defense benchmarks are the same as the previous spread, but with Modest, Pelipper will always OHKO Mega Delphox and low-bulk Sneasler with Rain-boosted Weather Ball and Hurricane, respectively. The Special Defense and HP SPs allow Pelipper to survive Moonblast from Timid Mega Floette. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Dragon-types like Mega Dragonite and Archaludon can easily tank a Weather Ball and retaliate with Draco Meteor, threatening Pelipper on its lower Special Defense. Opposing weather also can be a struggle, forcing Pelipper to swap out and possibly leaving it open to be predicted and KOed when attempting to swap back in. Mega Pokemon that set weather, notably Mega Tyranitar, Mega Froslass, and Mega Charizard Y, handily override Pelipper's rain mid-turn via Mega Evolution. Rotom-Wash resists both of Pelipper's STAB moves and can OHKO it with Thunderbolt, while Milotic can tank attacks and put it to sleep with Hypnosis, leaving it open to be KOed. Good Partners and Synergies: Archaludon is very helpful against Dragon- and Water-types that annoy Pelipper, hitting them hard with Draco Meteor and Electro Shot, respectively. Additionally, it threatens Mega Tyranitar, Mega Froslass, and Alolan Ninetales with Flash Cannon, all of which are opposing weather setters. Basculegion-M can also threaten opposing weather setters, even having the capability of OHKOing Mega Charizard Y in the sun with Wave Crash. In return, Pelipper can set up the rain for both of these, allowing Archaludon's Electro Shot and Basculegion's Wave Crash to become far more threatening. Mega Venusaur threatens Pokemon like Mega Floette that may hit Pelipper for big damage and the Water-types that disrupt it. Thanks to Pelipper, Mega Venusaur can sit in the rain to avoid being majorly threatened by Fire-type attacks from the likes of Mega Charizard Y and Incineroar. Mega Lopunny can more freely fire off attacks due to Pelipper's ability to threaten Pokemon like Sneasler and Mega Scovillain, all while providing Fake Out support to protect Pelipper when switching in. Incineroar also provides Fake Out support as well as a slower pivot option with Parting Shot, getting Pelipper in safely. How to Overcome Pelipper's Weaknesses: Attempting to remove opposing weather should be a top priority to allow Pelipper to shine. Partners like the occasional Mega Scovillain, Sinistcha, and Incineroar provide redirection, slow pivoting, and Fake Out support, all of which allow Pelipper to swap in safely. Pelipper should most often be brought in the back against opposing weather teams to try and ensure that rain will be the dominant weather. Against non-weather teams, leading Pelipper with a strong rain Pokemon like Basculegion-M can apply lots of pressure early-game.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
+{"species":"incineroar","moves":["fakeout","flareblitz","partingshot","throatchop"],"item":"sitrusberry","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":32,"atk":0,"def":20,"spa":0,"spd":12,"spe":2},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:18Z","rationale":"Incineroar is the premier support Pokemon in all of VGC, using Fake Out to deny attacks and constantly cycling said Fake Out while also applying offense stat drops with Parting Shot and Intimidate to gain control of the damage on the board. Thanks to its good natural bulk and exceptional typing, it can switch in with ease on attacks such as the powerful Ghost-type onslaught from the likes of Aegislash and Mega Gengar as well as Fire-types like Mega Charizard Y. Flare Blitz: Flare Blitz allows Incineroar to make use of its high Attack stat, preventing foes from ignoring it especially when the sun is up. Flare Blitz notably threatens Kingambit, which usually scares away Incineroar due to Defiant. Throat Chop versus Darkest Lariat: Throat Chop deals a decent chunk of damage while also preventing sound-based moves; this means common moves such as Sylveon's and Primarina's Hyper Voice and opposing Incineroar's Parting Shot are blocked. Darkest Lariat, on the other hand, ignores Archaludon's Stamina boosts, though that is rather irrelevant in most matchups; the most enticing part of Darkest Lariat is its higher Base Power, allowing Incineroar to always 2HKO Shield forme Aegislash. Parting Shot: Parting Shot allows Incineroar to continuously lower foes' damage. Using Parting Shot also typically allows Incineroar to switch out last due to its low Speed, allowing frailer allies like Sneasler to come in safely. Other Options Protect: By dropping one of Incineroar's STAB attacks, Protect allows it to better position itself around the board. This is almost exclusively seen on Perish trap teams, where Incineroar plays a key role of in using Fake Out and Protect to stall Perish Song turns and then using Parting Shot to pivot around. Will-O-Wisp: Will-O-Wisp allows Incineroar to further weaken physical attacks on top of Intimidate and allows Incineroar to cripple Pokemon immune to Intimidate, namely Defiant Kingambit and Inner Focus Dragonite. Taunt: Taunt notably allows Incineroar to deny Trick Room from Farigiraf and Sinistcha, as well as crippling Pokemon that thrive off of their status moves, especially Whimsicott and Sableye. Items Sitrus Berry: Sitrus Berry grants Incineroar more sustainability, allowing it to avoid a 2HKO from Modest Mega Dragonite's Hurricane and almost always avoiding a 2HKO from Timid offensive Mega Floette's Moonblast. Chople Berry: While this is mostly used when Sitrus Berry has already been taken, Chople Berry still has its uses; most notably, it allows Incineroar to survive Close Combat from Sneasler, though only once. Abilities Intimidate: Intimidate is what makes Incineroar so strong; it can switch in, drop the foes' Attack stat, switch out, and repeat that same process over and over again. SP Spreads 32 HP / 20 Def / 12 SpD / 2 Spe with Careful: With the given bulk, Incineroar, after an Intimidate, survives Wave Crash from Adamant Adaptability Basculegion-M. Additionally, it avoids a 2HKO from Timid Mega Gengar's Sludge Bomb, and it survives Hyper Beam from Sylveon and Light of Ruin from Mega Floette. The Speed investment allows Incineroar to outspeed non-invested Tyranitar, possibly letting it get off a crucial Parting Shot. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Incineroar is very prone to the rain teams that plague the format, where Pokemon such as Pelipper, Politoed, and Basculegion-M can all OHKO Incineroar when they're in the rain. Incineroar also struggles against White Herb Sneasler and the less popular White Herb Garchomp, since thanks to White Herb, they can ignore Intimidate and fire back with Close Combat and Stomping Tantrum or Earthquake, respectively. Incineroar also struggles against Rock-type Pokemon due to its inability to damage them hard; these Rock-type Pokemon include Mega Tyranitar, Mega Aerodactyl, Mega Glimmora, and their base formes. Good Partners and Synergies: Sinistcha is a great defensive partner for Incineroar, switching into the many Fighting-, Ground-, and Water-type moves that would threaten Incineroar, while Incineroar can switch into the Fire-, Dark-, and Ghost-type attacks that threaten Sinistcha. Sinistcha also provides Trick Room for Incineroar to circumvent its relatively low Speed. Offensively, Basculegion-M threatens the many Rock-types that beat Incineroar and opposing Fire-types as well as walling most Sneasler out entirely. Mega Gengar can do the same to Sneasler, while Mega Aerodactyl can outspeed and OHKO Sneasler with Dual Wingbeat. Mega Charizard Y can prevent Pelipper and Politoed from hitting Incineroar with a super effective Weather Ball and can threaten Water-types with Solar Beam. Incineroar also functions well on Perish trap teams, working as a slow pivot that can use Fake Out to prevent foes from doing much while their Perish Song counters ticks down. How to Overcome Incineroar\u2019s Weaknesses: Bringing Pokemon that heavily benefit from the support Incineroar provides, such as Calm Mind Mega Floette and Mega Charizard Y, allows you to use it to its full potential; these Pokemon deal huge damage, compensating for Incineroar's passivity and allowing your team to break through would-be threats like Garchomp and Archaludon. Building your team with some way to circumvent rain is also advised, as this lets Incineroar avoid the powerful Water-type Weather Ball from Pelipper. Mega Charizard Y is the most effective, as it also matches up well against Sneasler that decide to forego Rock Slide; however, Incineroar doesn't typically fit on Mega Charizard Y structures due to stacking of Fire-types, so alternative weathers from the likes of Mega Froslass and Mega Tyranitar can also work.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a","nature":"Careful"}
