+{"species":"charizardmegay","moves":["heatwave","protect","solarbeam","weatherball"],"item":"charizarditey","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":14,"atk":0,"def":28,"spa":11,"spd":0,"spe":13},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:38:30Z","rationale":"Mega Charizard Y is very popular in the M-A metagame and has incredible sweeping potential due to its monstrous Special Attack stat and ability to set up sun. It can punish common Steel- and Grass-type foes such as Kingambit, Aegislash, and Sinistcha with its sun-boosted Fire-type attacks. Sun-Affected Moves: Mega Charizard Y's sun helps it secure OHKOs against foes such as Kingambit, Aegislash, and Sinistcha with Heat Wave. Weather Ball takes advantage of sun by becoming another Fire-type STAB move that deals immense damage against specially defensive foes such as Mega Floette, Farigiraf, and Mega Gardevoir. Weather Ball also adapts to opposing weather, punishing foes like Maushold with a 2HKO in rain. The move can also get around Wide Guard from the likes of Aegislash and Aerodactyl. Solar Beam helps Mega Charizard Y threaten Water-type foes such as Milotic and Basculegion-M, OHKOing non-bulky Basculegion-M sets and 2HKOing Milotic and Garchomp. Weather Wars: Mega Charizard Y can't always set up sun safely, as foes such as Alolan Ninetales, Mega Froslass, Pelipper, and Mega Tyranitar can change the weather upon switching in. Positioning is very important for Mega Charizard Y so that it can make full use of sun and not worry about it being overridden. Aside from switching Mega Charizard Y out, waiting to Mega Evolve is an optimal choice to prevent losing the weather war. Abilities Drought: Drought sets up sun, supporting partners by activating abilities like Venusaur's Chlorophyll and supporting Mega Charizard Y itself by increasing the damage of its Fire-type attacks and weakening Water-type attacks from threats like Milotic. Drought often forces opposing weather teams to remove Mega Charizard Y before executing their plans so they can maintain their weather. Drought can thwart opposing weather like rain by hindering the offensive capabilities of Swift Swim Basculegion-M and Pelipper. Mega Froslass struggles to set up Aurora Veil or rely on Blizzard in the face of Mega Charizard Y's Drought. SP Spreads 14 HP / 28 Def / 11 SpA / 13 Spe with Modest: The bulk on this spread allows Mega Charizard Y to survive Rock Slide from the likes of Excadrill and Garchomp, as well as Wave Crash from Basculegion-M as long as sun is active. The Special Attack SPs help it OHKO Basculegion-M with Solar Beam and give it a chance to OHKO Aegislash in Shield forme as well as Mega Froslass with Heat Wave. The Speed investment allows Mega Charizard Y to outspeed Adamant Swift Swim Basculegion-M while supported by Tailwind. 24 HP / 14 Def / 11 SpA / 17 Spe with Modest: This spread offers more Speed than the previous spread while still giving Mega Charizard Y a good chance to survive Rock Slide from Excadrill and Garchomp, though it is not guaranteed. The HP investment allows it to avoid a 2HKO from Mega Gengar's Shadow Ball and almost always avoid a 2HKO from Rotom-Wash's Hydro Pump while in sun. The Speed helps it move before Archaludon and non-maximum Speed Rotom formes. 2 HP / 32 SpA / 32 Spe with Timid: This spread maximizes Mega Charizard Y's Speed and Special Attack. The Speed investment helps Mega Charizard Y outspeed maximum Speed Glimmora as well as maximum Speed Rotom formes while guaranteeing at least a Speed tie with Mega Kangaskhan. The Special Attack investment allows Weather Ball to 2HKO Mega Floette in sun. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: While all Pokemon that change the weather away from sun prove to be a problem for Mega Charizard Y, it struggles most against Mega Tyranitar's sand and Pelipper's rain. Mega Tyranitar's sand enables Sand Rush Excadrill to move before Mega Charizard Y and threaten it with Rock Slide, while Mega Charizard Y struggles to deal any significant damage to Mega Tyranitar in sand and can be OHKOed by its Rock Slide in return. Pelipper's rain enables Swift Swim Basculegion-M, which threatens Mega Charizard Y with its Water-type moves. Rain can also grant Pelipper a Water-type Weather Ball, which is enough to OHKO Mega Charizard Y. Choice Scarf Garchomp and Aerodactyl can threaten Mega Charizard Y with Rock Slide, with Aerodactyl being able to OHKO it. Glimmora can easily threaten Mega Charizard Y with Power Gem and is a great pick in mirror matchups against opposing Mega Charizard Y. Opposing Glimmora and bulky Garchomp have an easier time threatening Mega Charizard Y under Tailwind. Good Partners and Synergies: Whimsicott can support Mega Charizard Y with Tailwind, helping it outspeed threats such as Choice Scarf Garchomp, and use Encore to put a foe into an unfavorable position, such as Sneasler after it uses Fake Out. Other Fairy-type partners like Mega Floette and Sylveon can threaten Dragon-type foes that resist Mega Charizard Y's Fire-type moves, such as Mega Dragonite and Garchomp, with Whimsicott and Sylveon not taking up a Mega slot. Aerodactyl has access to Tailwind and Wide Guard to prevent Rock Slide spam, as well Unnerve, which can help it deal with Sitrus Berry Basculegion-M and Garchomp sets. Garchomp can help against opposing Garchomp with Dragon Claw and opposing Mega Charizard Y with Rock Slide. It can also use Earthquake next to Mega Charizard Y to threaten Rock-type foes such as Mega Tyranitar, Hisuian Arcanine, and both Mega and base Glimmora. Basculegion-M is a strong sweeper that can handle opposing Fire-types such as Mega Delphox with Wave Crash or Aqua Jet, while Last Respects can be used as a general sweeping option due to its immense damage output. Kingambit helps in the Trick Room matchup due to its low Speed and can also threaten Basculegion-M with Sucker Punch and Farigiraf with Kowtow Cleave. Sneasler pairs well with Mega Charizard Y, as it provides Fake Out support and can threaten Rock-type foes such as Mega Tyranitar and Lycanroc-D with Close Combat. Incineroar also provides Fake Out and Intimidate support against foes such as Basculegion-M and Garchomp, and it can threaten Basculegion-M with Throat Chop or Darkest Lariat. How to Overcome Mega Charizard Y's Weaknesses: Leading with Mega Charizard Y can be risky against opposing weather teams, as it could easily lose weather control. Keeping it in the back can help position around opposing weather set by Pelipper and Mega Tyranitar. Eliminating opposing weather setters can put Mega Charizard Y in an advantageous position, as it no longer has to worry about sun being removed. Waiting to Mega Evolve until opposing weather has already been set up can also grant it a favorable position. It does, however, struggle to damage Dragon-type foes such as Dragonite, Garchomp, and Archaludon in rain. Having a Fairy- or Dragon-type partner such as Sylveon, Mega Floette, or Garchomp can help against the aforementioned foes. Mega Floette is great at covering matchups that Mega Charizard Y struggles against, such as Mega Tyranitar and Dragonite teams. Basculegion-M can help against Rock-type threats like Mega Tyranitar, Hisuian Arcanine, and Aerodactyl with its Water-type moves. Opposing Tailwind can cause foes such as Mega Tyranitar and Glimmora to outspeed Mega Charizard Y. Whimsicott can counter opposing Tailwind with a Tailwind of its own or by using Taunt on the opposing Tailwind user. Dual screens from foes like Sableye can also cause Mega Charizard Y and its partners to lose momentum. Stalling out screen turns with Protect or switching is an option to get around them. Protect and switching around are ways to avoid Fake Out cycling aimed at Mega Charizard Y.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a","nature":"Modest"}
+{"species":"whimsicott","moves":["encore","moonblast","protect","tailwind"],"item":"focussash","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":18,"atk":0,"def":6,"spa":10,"spd":0,"spe":32},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:19Z","rationale":"Whimsicott is able to provide crucial Tailwind support to many Pokemon that rely on it, such as Mystic Water Basculegion-M, offensive Mega Floette, and bulky Garchomp. At the same time, it can shut down almost any attempts at setting up with Encore, making Whimsicott ideal for both support and disruption. Encore: While Tailwind teams can sometimes struggle with setup Pokemon or moves such as Fake Out, Encore attaches a risk to these moves by forcing foes to switch out or prevent Encore in other ways risk being locked into a useless move. It synergizes well with Whimsicott's Grass typing, which allows it to ignore Rage Powder from Sinistcha attempting to redirect Encore. Encore can also be used to lock foes into resisted attacks, such as opposing Basculegion-M's Wave Crash instead of Last Respects, protecting Whimsicott and potentially its partner. Items Focus Sash: Focus Sash allows Whimsicott to run more Speed but means it cannot be as offensive as Fairy Feather variants, as quite a few SPs are still required in bulk to survive Dual Wingbeat from Aerodactyl. Fairy Feather: Fairy Feather allows Whimsicott to threaten a 2HKO on bulky Sitrus Berry Garchomp with Moonblast while leaving room for a lot of SPs in bulk. This item is mostly used when Focus Sash is used by a different Pokemon. SP Spreads 18 HP / 6 Def / 10 SpA / 32 Spe with Timid: This spread is only run by Focus Sash variants. Whimsicott will survive Dual Wingbeat from Jolly Aerodactyl almost the entire time, with the rest of the SPs prioritized towards Speed in order to Speed tie other Whimsicott. 30 HP / 17 Def / 9 SpA / 10 SpD with Bold: This spread is only run by Fairy Feather variants, as Focus Sash variants do not need to hit as many benchmarks. Whimsicott is guaranteed to survive Iron Head from Adamant-natured Kingambit and will almost always survive Moonblast from Mega Floette. The offensive investment allows Whimsicott to almost always 2HKO bulky Sitrus Berry Garchomp. 2 HP / 32 SpA / 32 Spe with Timid: When combined with Mega Floette's Fairy Aura, this SP spread allows Whimsicott to deal a large amount of damage. Whimsicott can afford to run this spread when holding Focus Sash, as Dual Wingbeat from Aerodactyl is the only attack it needs bulk to survive. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: While Whimsicott is almost never totally useless, as Encore will almost always achieve something, it struggles against Pokemon like Mega Charizard Y and Mega Delphox, as they usually don't mind being Encored and resist Moonblast from Whimsicott. Venusaur, in a similar vein, resists Moonblast and is not bothered by Encore too much. However, Moonblast can break Venusaur's Focus Sash, so attacking Venusaur can still be worthwhile. Additionally, Incineroar can pose problems for Whimsicott due to its immunity to Encore, that allows it to shrug off Moonblast, and access to Fake Out to stop Whimsicott from using Encore. Good Partners and Synergies: While there are many Pokemon that appreciate Whimsicott's support and improve its performance in return, Mega Floette stands out as one of the best. Tailwind allows Mega Floette to outspeed nearly every other Pokemon barring other Tailwind-supported threats, while Fairy Aura allows Whimsicott to deal a surprisingly large amount of damage with Moonblast. Encore also helps Mega Floette beat foes it can struggle with, such as setup Corviknight and Aurora Veil Mega Froslass. Mega Charizard Y is another good partner, as Whimsicott's Tailwind lets even bulky variants outspeed Garchomp, one of Mega Charizard Y's biggest threats. Garchomp resists Weather Ball and Heat Wave, and Solar Beam isn't strong enough to take it out, but Whimsicott can bring Garchomp in range of Weather Ball with Moonblast. Garchomp itself is also a great partner, as it has a very good matchup against Mega Charizard Y and Mega Delphox, which Whimsicott struggles with. How to Overcome Whimsicott's Weaknesses: When using Whimsicott, well-placed Encores can get its team out of many bad positions. Leveraging Whimsicott's presence on the field with Protect is also important for this, as the opponent may feel locked out of several moves while Whimsicott is present. Moves such as Aqua Jet and Aurora Veil, as well as Earth Power from Venusaur, are examples of options that require positioning around Encore. Certain moves are only used in specific situations, such as when Venusaur uses Earth Power against Glimmora or when Aqua Jet picks off a foe at low HP, while others, such as Protect, can render a Pokemon useless if it is forced to use them multiple turns in a row. As such, these moves require much more precise positioning when Whimsicott is on the field. Leveraging this pressure alongside Whimsicott's Protect and the opportunity cost of manually switching can exponentially increase Whimsicott's usefulness. The partners mentioned in the previous section can help cover Whimsicott's common switch-ins, with Garchomp beating Incineroar using Earthquake or Stomping Tantrum as well as Mega Delphox and Mega Charizard Y using Rock Slide. When facing opposing Tailwind teams, using defensive options such as an allied Incineroar or bulky Pokemon may be more consistent than attempting to win Speed ties against opposing Whimsicott and lock it into Tailwind.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a","nature":"Timid"}
