+{"species":"pelipper","moves":["hurricane","protect","tailwind","weatherball"],"item":"sitrusberry","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":31,"atk":0,"def":14,"spa":0,"spd":18,"spe":3},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:19Z","variants":[{"hp":31,"atk":0,"def":14,"spa":2,"spd":16,"spe":3}],"rationale":"Pelipper is the premier rain setter of Regulation M-A. Being on the slower side, it is able to lead against opposing weather setters like Alolan Ninetales and have the rain override whatever the opposing weather is. Thanks to its Flying typing, it can threaten Grass-types like Sinistcha that otherwise spell trouble for rain teams. Weather Ball: Weather Ball threatens foes like Incineroar in rain, though it is useful in other weathers as well. For example, if the snow is set up, Weather Ball is capable of OHKOing opposing Garchomp. Hurricane: Hurricane is fully accurate in the rain, putting pressure on Grass-types like Sinistcha. Pelipper must be wary of using Hurricane, however, as it becomes rather unreliable if an opposing weather setter overrides the rain with their weather. It is also possible to use Hurricane to fish for confusion for a win, though this isn't to be relied on unless it is the only way out. Protect versus Wide Guard: Protect allows Pelipper to better position itself, setting up the rain more easily. Wide Guard, on the other hand, protects Pelipper and its partners from powerful spread moves such as Rock Slide from Mega Aerodactyl and Dazzling Gleam from Mega Floette. Weather Wars: Because of the abundance of weather within Regulation M-A, you must be careful when using Pelipper. Keeping it in the back to obtain weather control late-game will make it far easier to win, while leading off with your weather and forcing the opposing weather setter to swap in can leave them vulnerable. Items Sitrus Berry: Sitrus Berry allows Pelipper to nearly always avoid being 2HKOed by Dazzling Gleam from Timid Mega Floette and Wave Crash from Adamant Adaptability Basculegion-M in the rain. Abilities Drizzle: Drizzle enables partners like Archaludon and Basculegion-M while also shutting down opposing weather from the likes of Mega Charizard Y and Mega Tyranitar. SP Spreads 31 HP / 14 Def / 18 SpD / 3 Spe with Calm: The given SP spread ensures Pelipper survives Moonblast from Mega Floette or 150-Base Power Last Respects from Jolly Basculegion-M while giving it a good chance to survive Power Gem from Timid Glimmora. The Speed SPs allow Pelipper to outrun uninvested Corviknight. 31 HP / 14 Def / 2 SpA / 16 SpD / 3 Spe with Modest: The Speed and Defense benchmarks are the same as the previous spread, but with Modest, Pelipper will always OHKO Mega Delphox and low-bulk Sneasler with Rain-boosted Weather Ball and Hurricane, respectively. The Special Defense and HP SPs allow Pelipper to survive Moonblast from Timid Mega Floette. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Dragon-types like Mega Dragonite and Archaludon can easily tank a Weather Ball and retaliate with Draco Meteor, threatening Pelipper on its lower Special Defense. Opposing weather also can be a struggle, forcing Pelipper to swap out and possibly leaving it open to be predicted and KOed when attempting to swap back in. Mega Pokemon that set weather, notably Mega Tyranitar, Mega Froslass, and Mega Charizard Y, handily override Pelipper's rain mid-turn via Mega Evolution. Rotom-Wash resists both of Pelipper's STAB moves and can OHKO it with Thunderbolt, while Milotic can tank attacks and put it to sleep with Hypnosis, leaving it open to be KOed. Good Partners and Synergies: Archaludon is very helpful against Dragon- and Water-types that annoy Pelipper, hitting them hard with Draco Meteor and Electro Shot, respectively. Additionally, it threatens Mega Tyranitar, Mega Froslass, and Alolan Ninetales with Flash Cannon, all of which are opposing weather setters. Basculegion-M can also threaten opposing weather setters, even having the capability of OHKOing Mega Charizard Y in the sun with Wave Crash. In return, Pelipper can set up the rain for both of these, allowing Archaludon's Electro Shot and Basculegion's Wave Crash to become far more threatening. Mega Venusaur threatens Pokemon like Mega Floette that may hit Pelipper for big damage and the Water-types that disrupt it. Thanks to Pelipper, Mega Venusaur can sit in the rain to avoid being majorly threatened by Fire-type attacks from the likes of Mega Charizard Y and Incineroar. Mega Lopunny can more freely fire off attacks due to Pelipper's ability to threaten Pokemon like Sneasler and Mega Scovillain, all while providing Fake Out support to protect Pelipper when switching in. Incineroar also provides Fake Out support as well as a slower pivot option with Parting Shot, getting Pelipper in safely. How to Overcome Pelipper's Weaknesses: Attempting to remove opposing weather should be a top priority to allow Pelipper to shine. Partners like the occasional Mega Scovillain, Sinistcha, and Incineroar provide redirection, slow pivoting, and Fake Out support, all of which allow Pelipper to swap in safely. Pelipper should most often be brought in the back against opposing weather teams to try and ensure that rain will be the dominant weather. Against non-weather teams, leading Pelipper with a strong rain Pokemon like Basculegion-M can apply lots of pressure early-game.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a","nature":"Calm"}
 {"species":"basculegion","moves":["aquajet","flipturn","lastrespects","wavecrash"],"item":"choicescarf","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":2,"atk":32,"def":0,"spa":0,"spd":0,"spe":32},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:20Z","rationale":"Basculegion-M is one of the strongest revenge killers in the metagame, able to function both with and without rain. Along with acting as an offensive pivot against opposing frail leads, Basculegion-M also offers the ability to constantly threaten an OHKO on several common threats and Mega Evolutions, such as Aerodactyl, Talonflame, Mega Tyranitar, and Mega Charizard Y. Basculegion-M also possesses relatively decent natural bulk, allowing it to survive the occasional neutral hit. Basculegion-M's bulk also lets it act as a late-game sweeper more reliably by making use of its allies to remove its checks in the early game or to ultimately expend enough of the opponent's resources to allow Basculegion-M to enter, outspeed, and then brute force stragglers through the use of Last Respects or Wave Crash. Last Respects: Last Respects's damage, when powered up by fallen allies, is often enough to OHKO most of the common non-Ghost-resistant metagame threats even after defensive investment. Still, it is generally recommended to avoid locking into the move before allies have started fainting to allow Last Respects to reach its coveted OHKO capabilities. 150 Base Power is a reliable goal to hit once Basculegion-M settles on the field, as Last Respects becomes able to OHKO common Water-resistant foes such as Pelipper and Rotom-Wash, which would otherwise survive a Wave Crash even under rain. Aqua Jet: Aqua Jet is used to allow Basculegion-M to finish off low-health targets and ensure damage when you're on the back foot, with the added benefit of allowing Basculegion-M to potentially evade Kingambit's Sucker Punch. Positioning: Flip Turn is Basculegion-M's primary tool to pivot and reset momentum. Basculegion-M largely prefers not being led with, due to the predictability of being locked into a move and lack of Base Power. Flip Turn, when combined with Choice Scarf, also allows Basculegion-M to put in some chip damage and reset unfavorable positioning before the opponent's Pokemon can move. The set thrives on picking off weakened foes, so priority should be placed on removing threats to Basculegion-M. Other Options Icy Wind: Icy Wind may be used as a speed control replacement over Aqua Jet, sacrificing a priority option for the ability to drop the foe's Speed before they can move and break Focus Sash with its chip damage. It also heavily chips Garchomp, which is particularly vulnerable to it in the early-game. If you opt for this move, greater caution should be exercised when positioning Basculegion-M, as it will offer very little damage while locked into the move and will also invite opposing switch-ins. Kingambit and Milotic are potentially catastrophic; not only do they resist Icy Wind, but it will also trigger Defiant and Competitive, respectively. Items Choice Scarf: A Choice Scarf will allow Basculegion-M to reach Talonflame's Speed and outspeed the most common options above base 100 Speed, such as Sneasler, Mega Floette, and Mega Froslass. Abilities Adaptability: Adaptability allows Basculegion-M to pick up several key OHKOs; Wave Crash threatens a reliable OHKO on Aerodactyl, its Mega Evolution, and Excadrill. Meanwhile, 150-Base Power Last Respects will reliably remove many common neutral targets, like Sneasler and Talonflame, and it also threatens a potential OHKO against most Mega Floette sets. SP Spreads Jolly: A Jolly nature may be chosen to outpace Mega Gengar and Mega Delphox, which would otherwise outspeed Basculegion-M and, in Mega Gengar's case, threaten it with super effective damage. This will also allow Basculegion-M to outspeed Aerodactyl and Talonflame, which allows Basculegion-M to potentially deny them from setting Tailwind. Adamant: An Adamant nature allows Wave Crash to OHKO uninvested Mega Floette as well as Maushold and 150-Base Power Last Respects to OHKO Mega Scovillain semi-reliably. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Priority attackers pose the biggest threat to Basculegion-M. Kingambit is at the forefront of said threats, as it can threaten Basculegion with Sucker Punch for an OHKO while often having enough bulk to survive a rain-boosted Wave Crash. Basculegion-M is also threatened by bulky Water-resistant pokemon, particularly Grass-types such as Mega Venusaur that can hit it for super effective damage in return. Archaludon poses a serious threat to Basculegion-M in the rain with its Electro Shot and high physical bulk supplemented by Stamina. Basculegion-M's Speed tier means it will often be outran by other Choice Scarf users, like Rotom-Wash, Garchomp, and Vivillion, and especially speedy threats such as Mega Aerodactyl, and it's also particularly vulnerable to foes' Tailwind if it's unmatched. Common sun leads, such as Whimsicott with its Prankster ability, can specifically pose an indirect threat to Basculegion-M, nullifying its Speed advantage or forcing weather control through Sunny Day. Good Partners and Synergies: Basculegion-M is part of a known core made up of Sneasler, Kingambit, and itself. Basculegion-M appreciates Sneasler's capability to remove threats such as Kingambit and Incineroar with its Close Combat while also offering Fake Out support to help Basculegion-M with repositioning. Sneasler can cover many of Basculegion-M's weaknesses while being a fine choice to lead, which Basculegion-M specifically tends to avoid. Archaludon walls Basculegion-M early-game but lacks Stamina's Defense boost, which makes it particularly susceptible to Sneasler leads. Kingambit rounds out the trio by discouraging Intimidate through its Defiant ability and threatening Farigiraf, which is immune to Basculegion-M's Last Respects, while also discouraging Trick Room thanks to its lower Speed tier. Kingambit also renders the matchup against opposing Choice Scarf Basculegion-M less volatile by pressuring them with Sucker Punch, letting Basculegion-M avoid a Speed tie. Basculegion-M appreciates Pelipper's ability to set rain, which boosts its damage output and enables it to outspeed faster Choice Scarf users by setting Tailwind. Also, Pelipper's Hurricane provides coverage against bulky Grass-types that may otherwise threaten Basculegion-M like Sinistcha and Venusaur. How to Overcome Basculegion-M's Weaknesses: Basculegion-M's primary weakness to priority and susceptibility to Intimidate and common sun leads means it greatly appreciates being with partners with access to pivoting moves such as Incineroar with Parting Shot, which through its typing and bulk can be an effective defensive pivot that can sponge hits, particularly from Dark-, Ghost-, and Grass-type attacks that threaten Basculegion-M. Redirection users such as Sinistcha and Mega Scovillain are also greatly appreciated as a way to keep Basculegion-M safe from priority such as Kingambit's Sucker Punch and any threatening single-target damage. Fake Out users like Sneasler and the previously mentioned Incineroar can buy the time for Basculegion-M to pivot out safely or get a key attack off. Basculegion-M overall greatly appreciates being paired with Tailwind users like Talonflame and Pelipper, as they allow Basculegion-M to overcome its weakness to faster Choice Scarf users, as well as avoiding the Speed tie with other Choice Scarf Basculegion-M. Weather control options such as Pelipper with Drizzle and Sableye with Rain Dance bolster Basculegion-M's power, allowing Wave Crash to score otherwise unattainable OHKOs on Pokemon like Sylveon while also counteracting the potential damage loss incurred from opposing sun teams. Allies like Sneasler and Garchomp, with their Fighting- and Ground-type coverage, respectively, help deal with Basculegion-M's biggest obstacle: Kingambit.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
 {"species":"basculegion","moves":["aquajet","lastrespects","protect","wavecrash"],"item":"mysticwater","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":16,"atk":24,"def":6,"spa":0,"spd":0,"spe":20},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:20Z","rationale":"Basculegion-M is able to output monstrous amounts of damage with the combination of Adaptability and its high-Base Power moves. This set takes advantage of its strong natural bulk to ease matchups that would otherwise heavily favor the opponent, such as Kingambit and Mega Gengar, while still maintaining offensive prowess through the use of Mystic Water or using Sitrus Berry to alleviate Wave Crash's recoil damage. Wave Crash: Wave Crash is Basculegion-M's most consistent STAB move, allowing it to threaten Trick Room setters such as Farigiraf, as Basculegion-M's bulk usually isn't enough to outlast Trick Room, while also OHKOing or 2HKOing most Pokemon. Crucially, it lets Basculegion-M OHKO Sneasler. In combination with its defensive typing, Basculegion-M can almost always serve as a band-aid for Sneasler-weak teams. Last Respects: This move allows Basculegion-M to function as a late-game sweeper, letting it OHKO most Pokemon in the format after two of its allies faint. However, it is also usable after just one ally has fainted, usually in situations where Wave Crash recoil needs to be avoided. At 100 Base Power, it is able to OHKO Pokemon such as non-Kasib Berry Sinistcha, Mega Froslass, and Sneasler. Items Mystic Water: Mystic Water allows Basculegion-M to reach OHKOs on important metagame threats such as bulky Mega Floette, bulky Mega Gengar, and medium-bulk Mega Charizard Y in the sun with Wave Crash. Sitrus Berry: Sitrus Berry allows Basculegion-M to heal enough to safely use Wave Crash instead of knocking itself out due to recoil damage. This can be important against Pokemon such as Kingambit, which would invalidate Wave Crash as an option after a Sucker Punch.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
