-{"species":"archaludon","moves":["dragonpulse","electroshot","flashcannon","protect"],"item":"leftovers","regulation":"champions-reg-ma","spread":{"hp":32,"atk":0,"def":1,"spa":5,"spd":25,"spe":3},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:28:22Z","rationale":"Archaludon is one of the main centerpieces of rain in Regulation M-A, capable of sweeping through entire teams while being exceptionally difficult to take down thanks to its immense physical bulk, Leftovers, Electro Shot, and Stamina. Electro Shot and Archaludon's typing give it a good matchup against Aerodactyl, Rotom-Wash, Talonflame, Mega Gengar, and Mega Blastoise. Electro Shot: Electro Shot is Archaludon's main way of setting up while threatening common Pokemon like Basculegion-M, Pelipper, and Aerodactyl. Positioning with Archaludon: Archaludon can switch into weaker attacks such as Aerodactyl's Dual Wingbeat and Fake Out from Sneasler and Incineroar to accumulate Stamina boosts. Archaludon should stay on the field as long as possible, as each Stamina boost makes it harder to take down. Other Options Draco Meteor: Draco Meteor can be used in place of Dragon Pulse to give Archaludon access to more firepower. This allows Archaludon to OHKO bulky Garchomp. Aura Sphere: Aura Sphere is used to check opposing Kingambit, with a 50% chance to OHKO it. At +2, Archaludon also OHKOes Mega Tyranitar in sand with Aura Sphere. Items Leftovers: Leftovers grants Archaludon more longevity, enabling it to clean up games more easily. Abilities Stamina: Stamina allows Archaludon to become nigh impossible to KO with physical attacks, letting it tank several Earthquakes from Adamant Garchomp and Low Kicks from Kingambit. SP Spreads 32 HP / 1 Def / 5 SpA / 25 SpD / 3 Spe with Modest: This SP spread allows Archaludon to outspeed Choice Scarf Basculegion in Tailwind and survive Jolly Sneasler's Close Combat. The remaining points are put into Special Attack and Special Defense to maximize Archaludon's damage output and special bulk. Teambuilding Checks and Weaknesses: Archaludon's main weakness is its rather lackluster Special Defense, leaving it vulnerable to moves such as Mega Charizard Y's Weather Ball, Sylveon's Hyper Beam, and Mega Dragonite's Draco Meteor. Despite Archaludon's great physical bulk, Sneasler can threaten to OHKO it with Close Combat. Archaludon is extremely exploitable without any Stamina boosts, and its reliance on rain can make it tough to use in weather wars against Mega Charizard Y. Good Partners and Synergies: Archaludon is typically found on rain teams along with Pelipper, Politoed, Sinistcha, and Basculegion-M. Pelipper and Politoed provide Archaludon with rain so it can set up with Electro Shot freely. Politoed is naturally bulkier and faster, whereas Pelipper has access to a Flying-type STAB move in Hurricane to threaten opposing Grass- and Fighting-types such as Sinistcha and Kommo-o. Pelipper also has access to supporting moves like Wide Guard to block moves such as Mega Charizard Y's Heat Wave and Mega Floette's Dazzling Gleam and Tailwind to allow Archaludon to outspeed Mega Aerodactyl and Choice Scarf Basculegion. Basculegion-M covers for Archaludon's weaknesses well, acting as a switch-in to Fighting-type moves such as Sneasler's Close Combat while threatening to OHKO most Garchomp with rain- and Adaptability-boosted Wave Crash. Basculegion-M also has the potential to OHKO frailer Mega Charizard Y through sun with Adaptability-boosted Wave Crash. Calm Mind Baton Pass Espathra can be utilized on rain compositions to give Archaludon an alternative way of setting up without being forced to use Electro Shot. Mega Dragonite acts as an alternative Tailwind setter for Archaludon while threatening to OHKO Sinistcha with Hurricane, preventing Sinistcha from using Life Dew or Trick Room. Sinistcha gives Archaludon maximum support through Life Dew and Hospitality recovery, Rage Powder redirection, and Trick Room speed control. Sableye gives Archaludon more bulk via dual screens while using moves such as Rain Dance, Encore, Disable, and Will-O-Wisp to disrupt foes. Rain Dance is used in weather wars, Encore can lock foes like Sneasler into Fake Out and Pokemon that may threaten Archaludon into Protect, Disable can prevent attacks such as Sneasler's Close Combat from threatening Archaludon and can force Choice Scarf users to use Struggle, and Will-O-Wisp can harm physical attackers. How to Overcome Archaludon's Weaknesses: Archaludon's weakness to Sneasler's and Kommo-o's Fighting-type STAB moves can be patched up by threatening these Fighting-types with Pelipper's Hurricane, redirecting attacks with Sinistcha's Rage Powder, or switching Basculegion-M or Sinistcha into Sneasler's Close Combat and Kommo-o's Aura Sphere. Basculegion-M can heavily damage Garchomp with Adaptability-boosted Wave Crash or Last Respects after two or three allies have fainted. Archaludon's mediocre Special Defense can be patched up with Sableye's Light Screen and Special Defense boosts from Espathra's Calm Mind. Archaludon needs rain to set up with Electro Shot; therefore, having a manual Rain Dance user like Sableye alongside setters like Pelipper is great for keeping weather control when facing Mega Charizard Y.","source_format":"champions/vgc-2026-regulation-m-a"}
