 {"species":"archaludon","moves":["dracometeor","roar","stealthrock","thunderbolt"],"item":"leftovers","regulation":"champions-reg-mb","spread":{"hp":32,"atk":0,"def":23,"spa":0,"spd":11,"spe":0},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:38:52Z","variants":[{"hp":32,"atk":0,"def":2,"spa":0,"spd":32,"spe":0}],"rationale":"Archaludon is one of the best walls in the entire game thanks to its incredible bulk and Stamina. With access to Stealth Rock, phazing support, and Draco Meteor, Archaludon can efficiently wear down lineups that lack dedicated checks to it. Thunderbolt versus Flash Cannon: Thunderbolt is Archaludon's best complementary move to Draco Meteor for its ability to hit key bulky targets Primarina and Corviknight, which comfortably check Archaludon otherwise, as well as hitting many other Steel-types neutrally. Flash Cannon is a more consistent damage source overall, but it doesn't help Archaludon against Steel-types like Corviknight, Scizor, and Aegislash. Roar versus Dragon Tail: Roar is the preferred phazing option for racking up Stealth Rock damage, as Pokemon immune to it are extremely uncommon, and it works through Substitute. Dragon Tail is more generally oppressive by dealing direct damage and phazing at the same time, and it additionally cannot be shut down by Taunt. However, Dragon Tail can make Archaludon struggle against Substitute users and does not work at all on Mega Floette, which can set up on Archaludon and threaten to snowball out of control. Mirror Coat: Archaludon's bulk makes it a viable user of Mirror Coat, which allows it to deal significant damage or completely take out targets such as Primarina and Mega Gengar. Sitrus Berry versus Leftovers: Sitrus Berry's burst recovery is more effective at helping Archaludon survive multiple hits from big attacks, including super effective ones like Garchomp's Earthquake. On the other hand, Leftovers is superior when Archaludon can capitalize long-term on Stamina boosts from favorable defensive matchups against foes such as Meowscarada and Mega Starmie. Other Options Foul Play: Foul Play gives Archaludon a direct option against difficult physical attackers like Aegislash and Scizor, especially if they try to set up with Swords Dance. Rest and Chesto Berry: Restoring Archaludon to full health after receiving several Stamina boosts can completely ruin physically offensive lineups that rely on attrition to beat Archaludon. Iron Head: Specially defensive Archaludon with Iron Head is a specific adaptation to check Calm Mind Floette. If going this route, Archaludon generally should run a Careful nature over Calm and Dragon Tail over Draco Meteor. SP Spreads All 2HKOs mentioned below factor in Archaludon having a +1 Defense boost from Stamina for the second hit. 32 HP / 23 Def / 11 SpD with Bold: With this spread, Archaludon is guaranteed to avoid the 2HKO from Jolly Basculegion-M's 150-Base Power Last Respects and, when using Sitrus Berry, Adamant Garchomp's Earthquake. Leftover SPs are put into Special Defense. 32 HP / 2 Def / 32 SpD with Calm: Maximum special bulk investment allows Archaludon to avoid the 2HKO from Timid Mega Gengar's Shadow Ball and Modest Primarina's Moonblast, assuming no Fairy Feather on the latter. This spread works particularly well with Mirror Coat, as Archaludon also has a significant chance to survive +1 Moonblast from offensive Mega Floette.","source_format":"champions/battle-stadium-singles"}
 {"species":"archaludon","moves":["dracometeor","flashcannon","stealthrock","thunderbolt"],"item":"whiteherb","regulation":"champions-reg-mb","spread":{"hp":2,"atk":0,"def":0,"spa":32,"spd":0,"spe":32},"source_tier":"champions_native_writeup","verified":false,"verification_context":{},"date_resolved":"2026-07-27T18:38:52Z","rationale":"Archaludon can perform consistently as an offensive lead thanks to Sturdy, which lets it take full advantage of its respectable Speed and high-power attacks. Thunderbolt: Thunderbolt provides strong overlapping coverage with Draco Meteor, in particular pressuring defensive mainstays Primarina and Corviknight. Flash Cannon versus Steel Beam: Flash Cannon is the safest option for a Steel-type STAB attack and is more than sufficient against the likes of offensive Mimikyu and Glimmora. However, Steel Beam's massive Base Power makes dealing with bulkier threats like Hippowdon and defensive Mega Floette much more viable, even if it comes at the cost of survivability. Aura Sphere: Aura Sphere helps Archaludon win the mirror matchup while avoiding Draco Meteor usage, keeping its Special Attack stable and making it less likely to become setup bait. Even specially defensive Archaludon has a high chance to be 2HKOed. Aura Sphere is also the best option for taking on Kingambit. Stealth Rock: The combination of Archaludon's Speed and Sturdy allows it to reliably set Stealth Rock. Roar: Roar can disrupt dangerous setup sweepers like Calm Mind Mega Floette and Swords Dance Mega Lucario. Timid versus Modest: Many offensive threats such as Adamant Mimikyu, Jolly Basculegion-M, Jolly Hisuian Samurott, and Jolly Mega Gyarados are at or just below Archaludon's positive-nature Speed tier. As such, a Timid nature is usually preferred. However, the power increase from a Modest nature is still worth considering; for example, it gives Archaludon a decent chance to OHKO offensive Mega Gengar with Draco Meteor. Other Options Dark Pulse: Dark Pulse is offensive 